<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Essential 2.2 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-vectors" component="essential" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vectors</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      To give directions from your school to the nearest market you need to say how far to go and also which way. A quantity that has both size and direction is called a vector. A quantity with size only, such as mass or temperature, is a scalar.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will tell vector and scalar quantities apart, write vectors using the correct notation and represent them on a Cartesian plane. You will add vectors, multiply a vector by a scalar and find the magnitude of a vector. Finally you will use a vector to translate an object and draw its image.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vectors are used wherever direction matters: in navigation by pilots and ship captains, in surveying land, and in describing forces and velocities in science.
    </p>
  </introduction>

